olume of gas at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fc02c133ee4753e28d2d3b68272f60597186815196147075bc530f44368281f" "basis" 1480 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*During a certain chemical reaction, the **volume** of gas $G$ **increases** with time. The **rate** of change of the volume of gas is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t,$ in seconds.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of gas* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *square of the time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}∝{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of gas *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fc02c133ee4753e28d2d3b68272f60597186815196147075bc530f44368281f" "basis" 1480 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041088/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-143035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/