statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *time* since birth. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}∝t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the height *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=kt.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/state.json" "file_sha256" "40ce5ccb897493f267102e46f4b83ad3aea2d99c8c511cf1b1fb5892ab51e2ab"} {"question" "q-38310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c09223fadc4ccb407aa131e7335da061733021b3c5a22dcce02c085d96801191"} {"question" "q-38310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/state.json" "file_sha256" "40ce5ccb897493f267102e46f4b83ad3aea2d99c8c511cf1b1fb5892ab51e2ab"} {"question" "q-38310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-kH,\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\sqrt{kH},\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=kH,\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt{t},\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=kt,\\,\\,k>0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/state.json" "file_sha256" "40ce5ccb897493f267102e46f4b83ad3aea2d99c8c511cf1b1fb5892ab51e2ab"} {"question" "q-38310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=kt,\\,\\,k>0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/state.json" "file_sha256" "40ce5ccb897493f267102e46f4b83ad3aea2d99c8c511cf1b1fb5892ab51e2ab"} {"question" "q-143035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "During a certain chemical reaction, the volume of gas $G$ increases with time. The rate of change of the volume of gas is proportional to the square of the time $t,$ in seconds, since the reaction started. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of gas at time $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/state.json" "file_sha256" "40ce5ccb897493f267102e46f4b83ad3aea2d99c8c511cf1b1fb5892ab51e2ab"} {"question" "q-143035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*During a certain chemical reaction, the **volume** of gas $G$ **increases** with time. The **rate** of change of the volume of gas is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t,$ in seconds.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of gas* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *square of the time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}∝{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of gas *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/state.json" "file_sha256" "40ce5ccb897493f267102e46f4b83ad3aea2d99c8c511cf1b1fb5892ab51e2ab"} {"question" "q-143035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c09223fadc4ccb407aa131e7335da061733021b3c5a22dcce02c085d96801191"} {"question" "q-143035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/state.json" "file_sha256" "40ce5ccb897493f267102e46f4b83ad3aea2d99c8c511cf1b1fb5892ab51e2ab"} {"question" "q-143035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=-kt,\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=-k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/state.json" "file_sha256" "40ce5ccb897493f267102e46f4b83ad3aea2d99c8c511cf1b1fb5892ab51e2ab"} {"question" "q-143035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}G}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2},\\,\\,k>0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/state.json" "file_sha256" "40ce5ccb897493f267102e46f4b83ad3aea2d99c8c511cf1b1fb5892ab51e2ab"} {"question" "q-143077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "In a controlled environment, the temperature $T$ of a food container decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the temperature. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the temperature of the container $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/state.json" "file_sha256" "40ce5ccb897493f267102e46f4b83ad3aea2d99c8c511cf1b1fb5892ab51e2ab"} {"question" "q-143077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*In a controlled environment, the **temperature** $T$ of a food container **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the temperature.** *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *temperature $T$* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the temperature $T.$* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{T}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the temperature *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/state.json" "file_sha256" "40ce5ccb897493f267102e46f4b83ad3aea2d99c8c511cf1b1fb5892ab51e2ab"} {"question" "q-143077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c09223fadc4ccb407aa131e7335da061733021b3c5a22dcce02c085d96801191"} {"question" "q-143077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/state.json" "file_sha256" "40ce5ccb897493f267102e46f4b83ad3aea2d99c8c511cf1b1fb5892ab51e2ab"} {"question" "q-143077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-kT,\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k{T}^{2},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}},\\,k>0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k{T}^{2},\\,k>0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/state.json" "file_sha256" "40ce5ccb897493f267102e46f4b83ad3aea2d99c8c511cf1b1fb5892ab51e2ab"} {"question" "q-143077" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{{T}^{2}},\\,k>0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/state.json" "file_sha256" "40ce5ccb897493f267102e46f4b83ad3aea2d99c8c511cf1b1fb5892ab51e2ab"} {"question" "q-143078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intensity $I$ of a light source with a motion sensor increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the distance $d$ from the source to the object detected by the sensor. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the intensity of the light source $I?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/state.json" "file_sha256" "40ce5ccb897493f267102e46f4b83ad3aea2d99c8c511cf1b1fb5892ab51e2ab"} {"question" "q-143078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **intensity** $I$ of a light source with a motion sensor **increases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the distance** $d$ from the source to the object detected by the sensor.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *intensity* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the distance.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}∝\\frac{1}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the intensity *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}I}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{d}^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/state.json" "file_sha256" "40ce5ccb897493f267102e46f4b83ad3aea2d99c8c511cf1b1fb5892ab51e2ab"} {"question" "q-143078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c092